"""Portlin first-boot setup.

Runs once on tty1 before the display manager, and gives the stick its identity
and its defaults: account, language, keyboard, time, network, security, the
desktop's look, drivers, services and storage. Optionally a new LUKS
passphrase too, so the person holding the stick owns the key rather than
whoever built it.

A full-screen curses program rather than a graphical one. This runs before X
on hardware nobody has tested, where a text UI on tty1 is the only thing
guaranteed to work. curses rather than a chain of whiptail dialogs because it
is still the standard library, and it is what lets setup be one screen with a
step list, a way back to any earlier answer, settings grouped where they can be
read together, and portlin's own colours on the Linux console.
"""

